### RULING on the stash (asked for explicitly)

  **It satisfies red-first in substance. It is not the backfill pattern.**

  The distinction that decides it: backfill is evidence that misrepresents a state —
  a log describing a failure that did not happen, or a "red" run against a tree that
  already had the fix. Neither occurred, and I did not have to believe anyone to know
  it: the recorded tree was genuinely pre-fix, and I reproduced both logs
  assertion-for-assertion from a pre-fix tree I built myself. A red's purpose is to
  prove the test has grip on unfixed code. That purpose is fully served here.

  The second purpose of test-first — that the test is designed from the spec rather
  than fitted to the implementation — is served in this instance by the amendment
  rather than by the ordering: behaviours 7 and 8, their scenarios and their
  assertions were written by the architect in 11d9f73, before any of this round's
  code existed. The builder implemented a test it did not get to design.

  Disclosure is better than round 1's and I credit it: `df73e9a`'s commit message
  says "Recorded red 07 and red 08 against the pre-fix tree (implementation
  temporarily reverted via git stash, per Order of work step 5)". That is durable,
  pushed, and reviewable — the limb round 1's aborted verify failed.

  Two qualifications, neither changing the ruling:

  a. The disclosure is durable about the MECHANISM and silent about the REASON.
     "per Order of work step 5" reads as compliance with a prescribed procedure;
     step 5 prescribes recording the reds first, not stashing a fix that should not
     have existed yet. A reader of the history alone would conclude the builder
     followed the order and stashed unrelated work in progress. The deviation itself
     — implemented first, caught itself — survives only in the ephemeral report.
     What would close it: one clause. "Implemented before recording; reverted via
     stash to record the true pre-fix reds."
  b. I could verify this one only because the fix happened to be textual, so the red
     logs quote pre-fix strings and betray their own tree. A subtler fix — a
     reordering, a changed comparison — would have left a log identical either way,
     and I would have had nothing but the builder's word. The verifiability here was
     luck, not design. That is the same control gap as round 1's O-2, and it is why
     that class's proposed target is a check or a hook rather than a resolution to
     disclose.

  Class status: this is instance 3 of the class opened in round 1's O-2 — officina
  bookkeeping or evidence ordering corrected by hand mid-flight, with disclosure
  depending on an agent remembering. (W-047 round 2, censor's verify, disclosed in a
  review log; W-046 round 1, builder's verify, disclosed nowhere durable; W-046
  round 2, builder's stash, disclosed in a commit message.) The trend is improving
  and the target stands: `verify`/`red` should record or refuse, not rely on memory.

