## 6. F-4 (BLOCKING) — `vendorDiagnostic` reads two `Turn.raw` keys; the vendor uses
##       four shapes. Three instances, one covered. Live-measured

  Root cause, measured on claude 2.1.280 with the streams separated:

    claude -p --output-format json --model gpt-6-astra <W-044 policy>   -> exit 1
      STDOUT (1285 bytes): a VALID JSON envelope —
        is_error: true, api_error_status: 404, subtype: "success",
        result: "There's an issue with the selected model (gpt-6-astra). It may not
                 exist or you may not have access to it. Run --model to pick a
                 different model."
      STDERR (78 bytes):
        [claude-code:unrecognized_model] {"model":"gpt-6-astra","query_source":"sdk"}

  Because stdout parses, `runClaude` does NOT take the `{stdout, stderr}` branch
  (claude-code.ts:142). It takes claude-code.ts:144-152, which sets
  `reply: parsed.is_error ? "" : …` — forced to `""` — and `raw: parsed`, an object
  with neither a `stderr` nor a `stdout` key. `vendorDiagnostic` (talk.ts:239-248)
  reads only those two keys, so it returns undefined. The operator loses BOTH the
  stderr string AND the envelope's own `result` sentence — which is the single most
  useful line available, and is already sitting in `raw`.

  The brief's behaviour-7 preamble diagnosed the mechanism as "`turn.reply` is `""`
  on exactly the paths where the vendor said something useful — claude-code.ts puts
  `{stdout, stderr}` in raw when no envelope parses". That is one of the paths, not
  all of them: `reply` is ALSO forced to `""` at :147 on a parsed `is_error`
  envelope, where raw is the envelope. The fix was built to an incomplete diagnosis,
  faithfully.

  ### Every instance, enumerated. I read all four `Turn.raw` constructions in each
  ### profile (grep "raw:" across claude-code.ts and codex.ts, eight sites total)

  COVERED by vendorDiagnostic today:
    claude-code.ts:142   raw: { stdout, stderr }            no envelope parsed
    codex.ts:239         raw: { stdout, stderr, timedOut }  timeout
    codex.ts:263         raw: { stdout, stderr }            normal non-zero close
  N/A by design (exit 3 returns earlier in performTalk, before the diagnostic branch):
    claude-code.ts:140, codex.ts:254   usage-limit paths

  NOT COVERED — the class:
    I-1  claude-code.ts:149  raw: parsed   parsed envelope with is_error true.
         LIVE-MEASURED above. This is the exact case the amended Acceptance names,
         and the only shape the real vendor produces for it. Highest value: it
         discards two diagnostics, one of them plain English.
    I-2  claude-code.ts:131  raw: { error: r.error.message }   spawn failure, exit 127
    I-3  codex.ts:246        raw: { error: err.message }       spawn failure, exit 127
         I-2/I-3 are the same shape in both profiles and close with the same read.
         `available()` guards the common case but does not make them unreachable
         (a spawn can fail after the probe succeeds).

  Measured for contrast, and this is what F-2's remedy DOES fix live:

    claude -p --resume <vanished id> --output-format json --model claude-opus-5 …
      -> exit 1, STDOUT 0 bytes, STDERR "No conversation found with session ID: …"
      stdout does not parse -> claude-code.ts:142 -> raw {stdout, stderr} -> CARRIED.

  So F-2's two live symptoms split: the resume symptom (round 1's smoke turn 2) is
  genuinely fixed; the mismatch symptom (round 1's art-lead case, the one the finding
  was named on) is not.

  ### Why this blocks

  1. An explicit Acceptance condition, written THIS round to incorporate F-2, is
     measurably unmet by the round that was dispatched to meet it. I ran the exact
     specified command and quoted the exact message, as the amended brief asks.
  2. docs/ADOPTION.md now states, for this exact named case, that a `claude` that
     doesn't recognize the model "exits non-zero (`[claude-code:unrecognized_model]`),
     and `talk` relays the exit code **and** the vendor's own captured diagnostic".
     Live, for that case, it relays the exit code and the requested model, not the
     vendor's diagnostic. A shipped, behaviour-asserted document claiming more than
     the code does is F-1's exact defect class, recurring inside F-1's own remedy.
  3. It is a class of three with one instance covered — the W-026 pattern my charter
     names, where fixing one instance at a time costs a round each.

  ### What closes it — small, one place, no new call site

  a. Widen `vendorDiagnostic(raw)` in packages/commands/src/talk.ts to the shapes the
     profiles actually produce: the envelope's own error text (`result` when
     `is_error` is true, plus `error`/`api_error_status` where present) and the
     `error` key of the spawn-failure shape — alongside the `stderr`/`stdout` it
     already reads. Same redaction, same 300-char budget, same never-parsed
     discipline, same single site. Note for whoever builds it: reading `result`
     *because* the envelope's structured `is_error` flag is set is not "branching on
     the diagnostic" — the brief forbids branching on the diagnostic's TEXT, and
     claude-code.ts:147 already branches on `is_error` itself.
  b. Stub fidelity, which is what let this through (Sol's round-1 target 5 asked this
     question exactly). Behaviour 7 scenario A's claude stub models the mismatch as
     "nothing on stdout, error on stderr, exit 1". The real vendor emits a full
     parseable envelope on stdout AND the stderr line. Give that scenario the
     measured shape above, and the red is honest: it fails today.
  c. One scenario for the `{ error: … }` spawn-failure shape, covering I-2 and I-3.

