  SUMMARY: 13 caught, 0 survived. git status --porcelain after: empty.

  Method note, recorded because it produced two false SURVIVED/NOT-APPLIED results on
  my first pass: both shipped texts are hard-wrapped, and behaviours 6 and 8 collapse
  whitespace before matching — correctly, since a phrase legitimately wraps across a
  prose line or a JSDoc `*` continuation. A naive literal mutation does not. codex.ts
  carries "web fetch/search" twice, once on one line (:41) and once wrapped across
  :55-56 as "web\n * fetch/search"; ADOPTION carries "paired with unbounded\nreads".
  R8 and R12 only bit once re-run against every site (R8', R12'). The tests are right;
  my first mutations were under-inclusive. Worth knowing for the next censor.

  Behaviours 1-6 re-run at HEAD: unchanged and green. Round 1's 30 mutations are not
  re-run — they target claude-code.ts's argv, codex.ts's policy constant and talk.ts's
  model/no-reply paths, none of which this round touched; R11/R12'/R13 re-cover the
  only round-1 territory the rewrite disturbed (behaviour 6's doc predicates).

## 8. Observations

  O-8  The traditio now carries step 0's three outputs (42ab547), per the amended
       wording, and I re-ran them in round 1 into W-046-review-1.log §2. Round 1's O-1
       is closed: the Acceptance names a real artefact and a real gate. I did not
       re-run step 0 this round — no vendor version changed and the constant is
       untouched.
  O-9  Behaviour 8's "no unqualified claim" predicate is a single exact-literal
       negative match on "no browser, in-app-browser, apps or plugin feature". It
       would not catch a differently-worded egress overclaim. It is load-bearing only
       in combination with the two positive assertions beside it, which do force the
       residual to be stated — so the block as a whole holds. Noted rather than filed:
       it is Sol's round-1 target 4 (literal matching over prose is brittle) in its
       mildest form, and a sharper predicate was not found by either of us.
  O-10 `extractHeaderComment`'s disclosed bug is real and the disclosure is the right
       kind. Left in place, the stray `*` from a wrapped JSDoc line would have split
       exactly the phrase behaviour 8 asserts absent, and the check would have passed
       falsely. The builder found it while drafting, fixed it, and said so in
       df73e9a's message. That is the disclosure standard the stash note (§2a) did
       not quite reach, in the same commit.
  O-11 The brief amendment 11d9f73 carries `Co-Authored-By: qa-lead (censor)`. I did
       not write, sign or co-author it. The trailer is presumably meant to credit the
       findings it incorporates, and nothing turns on it here — but my lex is explicit
       that specs are signed by the architect and the censor's input is advisory, not
       gate-signing (P-008). A co-author trailer naming the reviewing sella on a spec
       could later be read as the censor having signed the spec it then reviews.
       Advisory; worth a convention decision, not a fix in this opus.
  O-12 Round 1's F-2 root cause is now partially shared with the filed claude-resume
       opus: the resume symptom's diagnostic IS carried after this round (§6), so
       that opus inherits a visible error message to work from, which is what the
       amended brief predicted it would need.

## 9. Verdict

  FAIL, round 2.

  F-1 and F-3 are closed, and closed well — the egress residual is named first,
  paired with unbounded reads, quoting W-044's refusal, in both shipped texts, with
  six assertions and four mutations holding it there; and the amendment carried the
  causal lesson into step 0 rather than leaving it in my log. F-2's remedy is a
  correct single-site guard, redacted and bounded, and it fixes one of that finding's
  two live symptoms.

  It does not fix the other, and that one is the case the finding was named on and
  the case the amended Acceptance names in writing. `vendorDiagnostic` reads two
  `Turn.raw` keys; the profiles construct four shapes, and the shape the real vendor
  produces for a mismatched model is not one of the two. I measured it live with the
  specified command and quoted the result. Three instances, one place to fix, one
  stub to give the shape the vendor actually emits.

  Dispatch F-4 as one change across all three instances — not I-1 alone. That is the
  whole reason the class is enumerated here.
